<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">19 August in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">8 events that happened on 19 August, from 43 BCE to 1991.</p></header>
<div class="tih-pinned-card"><p class="tih-pinned-label">International day</p><p class="tih-pinned-title">World Humanitarian Day</p><p class="tih-pinned-desc">Established by the UN General Assembly to mark the 2003 Baghdad bombing that killed 22 aid workers, World Humanitarian Day pays tribute to humanitarian workers killed or injured in the line of duty and calls for the protection of civilians in conflict.</p></div>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/rome" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rome.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/43-bce">43 BCE</a> <span class="tih-ago">2,068 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Octavian becomes consul after marching on Rome</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">When the Senate refused his demand for the vacant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a>, Octavian, Caesar's heir, marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> unopposed and, aged 19, became consul with his relative Quintus Pedius. Pedius set up a special tribunal that convicted Caesar's assassins in absentia.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">14 CE <span class="tih-ago">2,012 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Emperor Augustus dies at Nola</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Augustus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s first emperor, died aged 75 after more than four decades in power. His stepson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> succeeded him, confirming the hereditary principle of the Julio-Claudian dynasty.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">797 CE <span class="tih-ago">1,229 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Empress Irene has her son Constantine VI deposed and blinded</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Supporters of the empress Irene seized her son, the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vi">Constantine VI</a>, deposed him and had him blinded. Irene took the throne herself, becoming the first woman to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> in her own name.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vi">Constantine VI</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/947">947 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">1,079 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The rebel Abu Yazid dies after his defeat by the Fatimids</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Abu Yazid, known as the Man on the Donkey, died of wounds after his defeat and capture by the Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Mansur in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hodna-mountains">Hodna Mountains</a>. His Ibadi rebellion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> had once threatened the Fatimid capital, al-Mahdiyya.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharijites">Kharijites</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hodna-mountains">Hodna Mountains</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1561">1561 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">465 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Mary, Queen of Scots returns to Scotland</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Widowed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Mary landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leith">Leith</a> after 13 years in France. She returned as a Catholic queen to a kingdom that had just adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1812">1812 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">214 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">USS Constitution defeats HMS Guerriere</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The American frigate Constitution dismasted and captured HMS Guerriere off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a>. Seeing shot rebound from her oak hull, sailors nicknamed her 'Old Ironsides'.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1953">1953 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">73 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Coup backed by the CIA and MI6 topples Iran's Mosaddegh</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Army units and paid crowds overthrew Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-mosaddegh">Mohammad Mosaddegh</a>, who had nationalised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>'s oil industry. The Shah's power was restored, and resentment of foreign meddling fed the 1979 revolution.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1991">1991 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">35 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Soviet hardliners launch a coup against Gorbachev</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Communist hardliners placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Mikhail Gorbachev</a> under house arrest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> and declared a state of emergency. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-yeltsin">Boris Yeltsin</a> led resistance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, the coup collapsed, and the USSR dissolved by December.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a></div></div></li></ol>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-born"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-born">Born on 19 August <span class="n">2</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-stuart-queen-of-bohemia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/elizabeth-stuart-queen-of-bohemia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Elizabeth Stuart, Queen of Bohemia</span><span class="tp">1596–1662</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/manuel-l-quezon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/manuel-l-quezon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Manuel L. Quezon</span><span class="tp">1878–1944</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-died"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-died">Died on 19 August <span class="n">5</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-holy-roman-emperor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederick-iii-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Frederick III, Holy Roman Emperor</span><span class="tp">1415–1493</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-jagiellon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/alexander-jagiellon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Alexander Jagiellon</span><span class="tp">1461–1506</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/andrea-palladio"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/andrea-palladio.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Andrea Palladio</span><span class="tp">1508–1580</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/balthasar-neumann"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/balthasar-neumann.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Balthasar Neumann</span><span class="tp">1687–1753</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/alcide-de-gasperi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/alcide-de-gasperi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Alcide De Gasperi</span><span class="tp">1881–1954</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<p class="day-ppl-note">Dates as the sources give them: in the Julian calendar before 1582, and in some countries for longer (Britain until 1752, Russia until 1918). Only people whose day of birth or death the records agree on are listed.</p>
